{"id": "136999", "revid": "195012", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=136999", "title": "Khopoli", "text": "Khopoli is an industrial town of 109,000 people (2011) in the Raigad district of Maharashtra, at the base of the Sahyadri mountains. Patalganga River flows through Khopoli. The city is called a city of waterfalls due to the number of waterfalls in the rainy season.\nUnderstand.\nClimate.\nKhopoli has a tropical monsoon climate (Am) with little to no rainfall from November to May and hefty rainfall from June to September with moderately heavy showers in October.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nKhopoli can also be reached by car on the NH4 about 80 km south of Mumbai. The Mumbai-Pune Expressway passes through the city."}
{"id": "137024", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=137024", "title": "Vegreville", "text": "Vegreville is a town of 5,700 people (2016) on the Yellowhead Highway in East Central Alberta, approximately 100 km east of Edmonton. It is home to the world's largest \"pysanka\" (Ukrainian Easter egg).\nUnderstand.\nIncorporated in 1906, Vegreville is primarily an agricultural community and has strong historical ties to the Ukrainian immigrant community.\nGrand Trunk Pacific's rail line (now Canadian National Railway's Vegreville Subdivision) from Lloydminster reached west through Vegreville to Edmonton by 1909. The GTPR was completed to Prince Rupert in 1914 and financially ruined by 1919. CN79458, a red caboose retired in 1993, is on display in Elks Park off 48th Street.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nHighway 16 (Yellowhead Highway) (Prince Rupert BC to Portage la Prairie MB); exit onto Highway 16A into town.\nBy train.\nPassenger service to Vegreville ended in 1975. The former station is now a restaurant and event space.\nNearby.\nMundare.\nMundare is a small town about 24 km west of Vegreville, near the intersection of Highway 15 and the Yellowhead Highway in the heart of Kalyna Country."}
{"id": "137026", "revid": "107092", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=137026", "title": "Lahn Valley", "text": "The Lahn Valley is a romantic river valley with many castles and old towns in beautiful nature.\nCities.\nNorth Rhine-Westphalia\nHesse\nRhineland-Palatinate\nUnderstand.\nBordered to the north by the Westerwald and the south by the Taunus, the river Lahn flows into the Rhine.\nGet around.\nThe roads that follow the river are collectively signed as the \"Lahn-Ferien-Stra\u00dfe\".\nThe \"Lahntalbahn\" rail link runs from Koblenz to Wetzlar\nThe lower section is navigable for river ships with locks along the way. A large part can be travelled by small motor boats.\nSee.\nAs well as the stunning scenery, there are 300 castles, fortresses, churches and other fortified building.\nDo.\nThe valley is a popular place for hiking, cycling and canoeing. The lower and middle sections of the river can also be used by small motor boats. If you drive by car along the Lahn in summer, you can take a refreshing dip in some lakes.\nDrink.\nThe local Lahntal wines are Lahntal as Middle Rhine wines.\nSleep.\nAs wells as hotels in towns there are a number of camping and caravan sites next to the river along the valley."}
